# Roast is a TypeScript package that talks to providers over raw fetch

Roast was a Go binary shipped through npm as six platform packages and a
launcher. Every Roast install is already a Node project dependency, so a Go
toolchain, per-platform builds, and the launcher add release machinery
without serving users. We rewrite Roast in TypeScript as one pure-JS npm
package, `2.0.0`, with behavior parity against Go commit `cd08f10` except
for named changes: no Windows support, no `completion` command, Node
`>=22.18`, and the trust hash in ADR-0003.

Runtime libraries (`yargs`, `zod`, `jsonc-parser`,
`@substrate-system/debug`) are bundled into one esbuild ESM file, so
installing Roast adds no transitive packages to a user's project, the same
property the Go binary had. Their license texts ship in a generated
third-party notice.

Inference uses raw `fetch` with one adapter per protocol (`chat`,
`responses`, `anthropic`) and Roast's own agent loop, not a provider SDK
or an LLM framework. The Go version used the Fantasy framework but had to
wrap or override much of it: it captured raw response bytes for run
records, removed headers the SDKs read from ambient environment variables,
replaced retry and tool-call-repair behavior, sized requests for the token
ledger before sending, and reserved a wrap-up turn. Official SDKs would need
the same overrides on two codebases; owning the wire layer puts all of it in
one place.

## Considered options

- Keep Go: no rewrite cost, but keeps the multi-package release and a second
  toolchain for a tool whose users all run Node.
- Official `openai` and `@anthropic-ai/sdk` SDKs: typed streaming for free,
  but both read ambient configuration and run their own retries, which Roast
  must defeat to keep its credential and capture guarantees.
- Vercel AI SDK: closest to Fantasy, but it hides the request bytes and the
  retry policy that the ledger and run records depend on.
# TypeScript

Applies to `src/`, `test/`, and `scripts/`. Notes for one area are in
that area's file (index in `AGENTS.md`).

## Tooling

- `npm test` builds `dist/cli.js`, then bundles and runs the tapzero
  suite; it does not typecheck. A new test module must be imported from
  `test/index.ts`. esbuild drops unused imports without resolving them,
  so only `npm run typecheck` catches a broken import.
- `npm run test:one -- test/<area>/<file>.ts` bundles one test module
  to `test/one-bundle.js` and runs it through tap-spec. Test modules
  cannot run directly under Node: their `.js` import specifiers only
  resolve once bundled. It does not typecheck; run `typecheck` too.
- Lint with `npm run lint`: it preloads `scripts/eslint-ts6.ts`, which
  gives eslint the TypeScript 6 API because typescript-eslint throws on
  the TypeScript 7 that `typecheck` uses.
- Node runs `scripts/` directly (type stripping), so they use only
  erasable syntax: no enums, namespaces, or parameter properties.
- `baseOptions` in `scripts/esbuild-options.ts` holds the esbuild
  options every bundle shares (text loaders for `.tmpl`, `.md`, `.sh`;
  `mainFields: ['module', 'main']`, which `jsonc-parser`'s UMD build
  needs); `scripts/bundle.ts` adds the `require` shim banner. A test
  that bundles code itself uses both.
- `npm run build` also writes `dist/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt`
  (`scripts/licenses.ts`) and fails, naming the package, when a bundled
  package lacks an allowlisted `license` field or a license file. A new
  runtime library must pass it; extending `ALLOWED_LICENSES` needs
  maintainer approval. `@substrate-system/debug` fails it today, so no
  `src/` module may import it yet. Runtime libraries are
  `devDependencies` (bundled); `dependencies` stays empty.

## Source conventions

- Keep source ASCII: write `\uXXXX` escapes. Some editing tools decode
  escapes into literal characters when writing; check with
  `grep -nP '[^\x00-\x7f]'` after writing a file. eslint `--fix`
  (quote-props) rewrites a quoted `'\u00e9'` object key into a literal
  non-ASCII identifier; put such keys in a const and use a computed key.
- eslint rejects control characters in regex literals
  (`no-control-regex`) and labeled statements (`no-labels`); scan char
  codes and use helper functions instead.
- Startup guards live in `src/startup.ts`, not `src/cli.ts`: importing
  the entry runs the CLI, so testable helpers sit in other modules.
- Module-level swappable functions use a mutable hooks object plus a
  delegating export (`terminalHooks`, `skillHooks.lookPath`), because
  tests cannot reassign an ESM `let` export. Swap the object's field
  and restore it in `t.cleanup`.
- Shared helpers; import these, never re-implement:
  `src/gocompat/` (`strings.ts`: Go TrimSpace/Ext/`%.2f`/strconv.Quote/
  byte-order sort and `truncateUtf8`; `duration.ts`: Go durations as
  bigint nanoseconds; `match.ts`: Go `path.Match`),
  `src/textsafe/textsafe.ts`, `src/fsutil/atomic.ts`
  (`writeFileAtomic`, options cover trust, credentials, and gate temp
  names and error messages), `src/git/git.ts` (the only git runner;
  pass the CLI context's `env`, it reads `process.env` only when none is
  given), `src/util/pool.ts` (`mapPool`).

## CLI core (`src/cli/`)

- Commands are `CommandDef`s (`command.ts`) listed in `commands.ts`;
  `run.ts` builds the yargs tree from that list plus `registerCommand`
  (tests), and calls the handler only after parsing succeeds.
- A handler gets a `RunContext` (cwd, env, signal, fetch, one shared
  `LineReader`) and throws `UsageError` / `ExitSignal` / any error; it
  never writes the error itself or reads process globals.
- A group with `subcommands` and no handler prints its help.
- yargs number parsing is off (`parse-numbers: false`). Declare an
  integer flag as `type:'string'` and parse decimal digits in the
  handler, throwing `UsageError` on a bad value (see `parseLimit` in
  `runs.ts`).
- Terminal detection and browser opening are swapped through
  `terminalHooks` (`terminal.ts`).
- With only the default `$0` command registered, yargs omits the
  Commands section from root help; it appears once a second command
  registers.

## Review command (`src/cli/review.ts`)

- End-to-end review tests use `settingsRepo` (`test/helpers/review.ts`):
  a temp repo with an uncommitted change, a config on a fake `chat`
  server, keys in env, `review.snapshot: false`, and approval via
  `roast trust --yes`. It chdirs into the repo.
- yargs keeps only the last occurrence of an array flag under
  `duplicate-arguments-array: false`; review slice flags are re-read
  from `ctx.argv` (`review-flags.ts`). Flags whose "changed" state
  matters have no yargs default (`undefined` = not given).
- The structured finder call offers `findings` plus the agent's
  `__roast_continue`; assert tool presence, not exact lists.
- Verification (step 17) runs only with `doVerify` and at least one
  finding. `verify` (`src/verify/verify.ts`) never throws; an abort
  becomes undecided verdicts, so the run still writes its record and
  exits 4. Its result types `notes` as `string[]|null` (Go parity), so
  CLI code after step 17 takes `VerifiedResult`, not `Result`.
- Verifier CLI tests (`test/cli/review-verify.ts`) put the verifier on
  its own profile and model and tell its requests apart by the
  `submit_verdict` tool. The default role key variable
  (`ROAST_VERIFIER_API_KEY`) backs any profile, so an invalid-key test
  must delete it from the env.
- The entry exits within the tick of the first SIGINT, and identical
  signals sent back to back coalesce, so a second-signal test needs a
  slow wind-down (a fake `git` that exits slowly on SIGTERM).

## Tests

- Use the `test` wrapper in `test/helpers/test.ts`, which adds Go-style
  `t.cleanup` (tapzero has no teardown hook). tapzero's `Test` has no
  `notOk`; write `t.ok(!x, ...)`.
- CLI tests run in-process with `runRoast` (`test/helpers/run.ts`),
  which makes a temp repo with an isolated env (`test/helpers/repo.ts`:
  `tempRepo`, `tempDir`, which also sets `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES`).
  `test/cli/process.ts` and `test/tooling/bundle.ts` spawn `dist/cli.js`,
  so they need a fresh `npm run build` (`npm test` does that).
- Test environments come from `isolatedEnv`, which starts from
  `cleanEnv(process.env)` (`test/helpers/env.ts`): every `ROAST_*`,
  `OPENAI_*`, `OPENROUTER_*`, and `ANTHROPIC_*` variable is stripped. A
  new provider whose key variable has another prefix goes in its
  `AMBIENT_PREFIXES`. Tests set credentials in `io.env`, never in
  `process.env`.
- A review test needs an approved `.roast.jsonc` on the fake server:
  `settingsRepo` approves, or call `approveProjectConfig`. A test
  asserting a config error approves a valid config and then edits it,
  so a trust failure cannot mask the error. A doctor test that probes a
  custom endpoint approves in its temp repo (`approveDoctorConfig`,
  `test/cli/doctor.ts`), never in this checkout.
- Untracked files are part of a review's diff, so a test that checks
  which file's text reached the fake server keeps decoys out of scope
  (for example through `.git/info/exclude`).
- Fakes: `startInferenceServer` (`test/helpers/inference-server.ts`)
  speaks the three protocols; `providerServer`
  (`test/helpers/provider-server.ts`) adds key-check and OpenRouter
  routes. `runRoast({ redirectTo })` uses `redirectingFetch`, so a
  profile on a built-in endpoint reaches the fake. `requestRecorder` and
  `reviewMeta` (`test/helpers/review.ts`) read what the fake received
  and the run's `meta.json`.
- Swap terminals and the browser with `simulateTerminals` /
  `captureBrowser` (`test/helpers/terminal.ts`).
- Tests that spawn processes use `test/helpers/spawn.ts` (`ROOT`,
  `run`).
- Git runner tests observe a child by putting a fake `git` shell script
  first on the injected `PATH` (spawn resolves the command through
  `env.PATH`); see `test/git/git.ts`.
- Live tests live under `test/live/` (entry `index.ts`,
  `npm run test:live`); each passes with a "skipped" note when its
  `ROAST_LIVE_*` key is unset, so the TAP stream is never empty.
# Gate

`src/gate/`, `src/cli/gate.ts`, `src/cli/gate-offer.ts`.

## Hook behavior

- The hook (`src/gate/pre-commit.sh`, imported as `HOOK_SCRIPT`) runs
  only `node_modules/.bin/roast` at the Git toplevel, never `PATH`. It
  first calls the hidden `roast gate settings` (prints
  `timeout_seconds=` and `severity=` lines from the config's `gate`
  section, defaults 180/HIGH), then `roast review --staged ...
  --max-severity <severity>`. Any failure, including a settings query
  that exits non-zero, warns and allows the commit; only review exit 1
  blocks.
- The hook runs from the Git toplevel without `--config`, so it sees
  only a toplevel `.roast.jsonc` (`gateConfigPath` in
  `src/cli/config.ts`). `gate enable` refuses and the review-time
  bootstrap stays silent without one; `gate enable` tests write a
  toplevel config first.
- Installed hooks are refreshed by content comparison with the script,
  so editing it needs no version bump.
- The config filename change makes hooks from older releases stale;
  refresh them when enabling the gate. `hook.ts` handles hook bodies
  as `Buffer`s so strip keeps every foreign byte. Hook
  writes use `fsutil` `writeFileAtomic` with `mode`.

## Tests

- Hook execution tests drive a real `git commit` with a shell stub that
  `newStub(t, repo...)` (`test/gate/exec-helpers.ts`) writes at each
  repo's `node_modules/.bin/roast`. It answers `gate settings` from
  `STUB_TIMEOUT` / `STUB_SEVERITY` and logs only the review call
  (`ARGS:` plus the staged diff), so `stub.ran` is empty when no review
  happened.
- Gate tests build repos with `gateRepo(t)` (`test/gate/helpers.ts`:
  global/system git config cut out, maintenance off, toplevel
  `.roast.jsonc`); hook execution tests use `seededRepo`, `newStub`, and
  `commit` from `test/gate/exec-helpers.ts`.
- Simulate a non-local `roast.gate` with
  `GIT_CONFIG_COUNT`/`KEY_0`/`VALUE_0`, never a global file.
- `ROAST_TEST_SKIP_GATE_TIMEOUT=1` skips the sleeping tests.
- The review-time offer (`src/cli/gate-offer.ts`, `src/gate/bootstrap.ts`)
  runs at the start of every `review` / bare `roast` with a toplevel
  config. Without terminals it records `roast.gate-notice=sent`; with
  `simulateTerminals(t, true)` it reads the first stdin line. A review
  test that feeds stdin to a later prompt (trust, keys) must first record
  `roast.gate` or set `gate.offer: false`, or account for the offer
  taking that line. Offer tests are in `test/cli/gate-offer.ts`.
// The installed hook driven through a real `git commit` (spec 12, 5,
// "Hook execution"), with a shell stub at node_modules/.bin/roast so no
// model is called. The hook is installed with `roast gate enable`, so
// these prove the TS install path writes a working hook.
//
// ROAST_TEST_SKIP_GATE_TIMEOUT=1 skips the tests that sleep.
import fs from 'node:fs'
import path from 'node:path'
import { test } from '../helpers/test.js'
import { runRoast } from '../helpers/run.js'
import { git, scratch, writeHook } from './helpers.js'
import {
    BLOCKING,
    CLEAN,
    commit,
    enable,
    logLine,
    newStub,
    projectRoast,
    seededRepo,
} from './exec-helpers.js'

const SKIP_SLOW = process.env.ROAST_TEST_SKIP_GATE_TIMEOUT === '1'

test('the gate reviews only the path-scoped commit content', async t => {
    const r = seededRepo(t)
    await enable(t, r)
    const s = newStub(t, r.dir)
    const { ok } = commit(r, s, { STUB_STDOUT:CLEAN })
    t.ok(ok, 'commit succeeds')
    const seen = s.ran()
    t.equal(logLine(seen, 'NAMES: '), 'b.txt', 'scope is exactly b.txt')
    t.ok(seen.includes('b-IN-THIS-COMMIT'), 'sees the committed content')
    t.ok(!seen.includes('a-STAGED-NOT-COMMITTED'),
        'does not see staged content outside the commit')
    t.equal(git(r.dir, r.env, 'diff', '--cached', '--name-only'), 'a.txt',
        'a.txt is still staged')
})

test('findings block the commit and reach the user', async t => {
    const r = seededRepo(t)
    await enable(t, r)
    const s = newStub(t, r.dir)
    const { ok, out } = commit(r, s, {
        STUB_STDOUT:BLOCKING,
        STUB_STDERR:'MARKER-R-abc123',
        STUB_EXIT:'1',
    })
    t.ok(!ok, 'commit blocked')
    t.ok(out.includes('MARKER-R-abc123'), 'the stub stderr is shown')
    t.equal(git(r.dir, r.env, 'rev-list', '--count', 'HEAD'), '1',
        'nothing committed')
})

test('ROAST_SKIP bypasses the gate entirely', async t => {
    const r = seededRepo(t)
    await enable(t, r)
    const s = newStub(t, r.dir)
    const { ok } = commit(r, s, { ROAST_SKIP:'1', STUB_EXIT:'1' })
    t.ok(ok, 'commit succeeds')
    t.equal(s.ran(), '', 'no review ran')
})

test('operational failures allow the commit', async t => {
    const cases:[string, Record<string, string>][] = [
        ['empty stdout, exit 0', { STUB_EXIT:'0' }],
        ['empty stdout, exit 3', { STUB_EXIT:'3' }],
        ['exit 4', {
            STUB_STDOUT:CLEAN,
            STUB_STDERR:'MARKER-unreachable',
            STUB_EXIT:'4',
        }],
        ['exit 2', {
            STUB_STDERR:'MARKER-untrusted', STUB_EXIT:'2',
        }],
    ]
    for (const [name, extra] of cases) {
        const r = seededRepo(t)
        await enable(t, r)
        const s = newStub(t, r.dir)
        const { ok, out } = commit(r, s, extra)
        t.ok(ok, `${name}: commit allowed`)
        t.ok(s.ran() !== '', `${name}: the review ran`)
        if (extra.STUB_STDERR) {
            t.ok(out.includes(extra.STUB_STDERR), `${name}: stderr shown`)
        }
    }
})

test('a missing project roast allows the commit', async t => {
    const r = seededRepo(t)
    await enable(t, r)
    // A roast on PATH must never stand in for the project one.
    const bin = scratch(t)
    const onPath = newStub(t, bin)
    fs.renameSync(projectRoast(bin), path.join(bin, 'roast'))
    const { ok } = commit(r, onPath, {
        PATH:`${bin}:${r.env.PATH ?? ''}`,
        STUB_EXIT:'1',
        STUB_STDOUT:BLOCKING,
    })
    t.ok(ok, 'commit allowed')
    t.equal(onPath.ran(), '', 'no roast from PATH ran')
})

test('failing gate settings allow the commit without a review',
    async t => {
        const r = seededRepo(t)
        await enable(t, r)
        const s = newStub(t, r.dir)
        const { ok, out } = commit(r, s, {
            STUB_SETTINGS_EXIT:'2',
            STUB_SETTINGS_STDERR:'MARKER-no-config',
            STUB_EXIT:'1',
            STUB_STDOUT:BLOCKING,
        })
        t.ok(ok, 'commit allowed')
        t.ok(out.includes('MARKER-no-config'), 'settings stderr shown')
        t.equal(s.ran(), '', 'no review ran')
    })

test('severity comes from gate settings, not the environment',
    async t => {
        const r = seededRepo(t)
        await enable(t, r)
        const s = newStub(t, r.dir)
        const { ok } = commit(r, s, {
            STUB_STDOUT:CLEAN,
            STUB_SEVERITY:'MEDIUM',
            ROAST_GATE_SEVERITY:'CRITICAL',
        })
        t.ok(ok, 'commit succeeds')
        t.ok(logLine(s.ran(), 'ARGS: ').includes('--max-severity MEDIUM'),
            'the review threshold is the settings severity')
    })

if (!SKIP_SLOW) {
    test('a review past the timeout allows the commit', async t => {
        const r = seededRepo(t)
        await enable(t, r)
        const s = newStub(t, r.dir)
        const started = Date.now()
        const { ok } = commit(r, s, {
            STUB_SLEEP:'10',
            STUB_STDOUT:BLOCKING,
            STUB_EXIT:'1',
            STUB_TIMEOUT:'1',
        })
        t.ok(ok, 'commit allowed')
        t.ok(Date.now() - started < 9000, 'the review was cut short')
    })

    test('the timeout comes from gate settings, not the environment',
        async t => {
            const r = seededRepo(t)
            await enable(t, r)
            const s = newStub(t, r.dir)
            const { ok } = commit(r, s, {
                STUB_SLEEP:'3',
                STUB_STDOUT:BLOCKING,
                STUB_EXIT:'1',
                STUB_TIMEOUT:'0',
                ROAST_GATE_TIMEOUT:'1',
            })
            t.ok(!ok, 'the review ran to completion and blocked')
        })
}

test('the appended block runs alongside a foreign hook', async t => {
    const r = seededRepo(t)
    const marker = path.join(scratch(t), 'foreign-ran')
    writeHook(r.hook, `#!/bin/sh\ntouch "${marker}"\n`)
    await enable(t, r)
    const s = newStub(t, r.dir)

    let res = commit(r, s, {
        STUB_STDOUT:BLOCKING,
        STUB_STDERR:'MARKER-R-deadbe',
        STUB_EXIT:'1',
    })
    t.ok(!res.ok, 'blocking review fails the commit')
    t.ok(res.out.includes('MARKER-R-deadbe'), 'findings shown')

    res = commit(r, s, { STUB_STDOUT:CLEAN })
    t.ok(res.ok, 'clean review commits')
    t.ok(fs.existsSync(marker), 'the foreign hook ran too')
})

test('the block runs in a foreign hook that exits early', async t => {
    const r = seededRepo(t)
    writeHook(r.hook, '#!/bin/sh\nexit 0\n')
    await enable(t, r)
    const s = newStub(t, r.dir)
    const { ok } = commit(r, s, { STUB_STDOUT:BLOCKING, STUB_EXIT:'1' })
    t.ok(!ok, 'commit blocked')
    t.ok(s.ran() !== '', 'the review ran')
})

test('a non-executable hook does not run', async t => {
    const r = seededRepo(t)
    writeHook(r.hook, '#!/bin/sh\nmake lint\n')
    await enable(t, r)
    fs.chmodSync(r.hook, 0o644)
    const status = await runRoast(['gate', 'status'],
        { cwd:r.dir, env:r.env })
    t.equal(status.code, 0, 'status exits 0')
    const s = newStub(t, r.dir)
    const { ok } = commit(r, s, { STUB_EXIT:'1' })
    t.ok(ok, 'commit succeeds')
    t.equal(s.ran(), '', 'git skipped the hook')
})
// The hook execution harness (spec 12, 5): a shell stub standing in for
// the project roast, seeded repositories, and real path-scoped commits.
import { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process'
import fs from 'node:fs'
import path from 'node:path'
import type { CleanupTest } from '../helpers/test.js'
import { runRoast } from '../helpers/run.js'
import {
    gateRepo,
    git,
    scratch,
    writeHook,
    type GateRepo,
} from './helpers.js'

// Ported verbatim from internal/gate/hook_exec_test.go.
export const STUB_SCRIPT = `#!/bin/sh
if [ "$1 $2" = "gate settings" ]; then
  if [ -n "\${STUB_SETTINGS_STDERR:-}" ]; then
    printf '%s\\n' "$STUB_SETTINGS_STDERR" >&2
  fi
  printf 'timeout_seconds=%s\\nseverity=%s\\n' \\
    "\${STUB_TIMEOUT:-180}" "\${STUB_SEVERITY:-HIGH}"
  exit "\${STUB_SETTINGS_EXIT:-0}"
fi
{
  echo "ARGS: $*"
  echo "NAMES: $(git -C "$(pwd)" diff --cached --name-only HEAD | tr '\\n' ' ')"
  echo "DIFF-START"
  git -C "$(pwd)" diff --cached HEAD
  echo "DIFF-END"
} >> "$STUB_LOG"
if [ -n "\${STUB_SLEEP:-}" ]; then sleep "$STUB_SLEEP"; fi
if [ -n "\${STUB_STDOUT:-}" ]; then printf '%s\\n' "$STUB_STDOUT"; fi
if [ -n "\${STUB_STDERR:-}" ]; then printf '%s\\n' "$STUB_STDERR" >&2; fi
exit "\${STUB_EXIT:-0}"
`

export const CLEAN = '{"findings":[]}'
export const BLOCKING = '{"findings":[{"severity":"HIGH"}]}'

export interface Stub {
    log:string;
    // The stub's observations, '' when no review ran.
    ran ():string;
}

export function projectRoast (repo:string):string {
    return path.join(repo, 'node_modules', '.bin', 'roast')
}

// Installs the stub as the project roast in each repo, one shared log.
export function newStub (t:CleanupTest, ...repos:string[]):Stub {
    const log = path.join(scratch(t), 'scope.log')
    for (const r of repos) writeHook(projectRoast(r), STUB_SCRIPT, 0o755)
    return {
        log,
        ran:() => fs.existsSync(log) ? fs.readFileSync(log, 'utf8') : '',
    }
}

// A repo with a.txt and b.txt committed, both modified, a.txt staged,
// and the gate consented to. The hook is not installed yet.
export function seededRepo (t:CleanupTest):GateRepo {
    const r = gateRepo(t)
    fs.writeFileSync(path.join(r.dir, 'a.txt'), 'a-committed\n')
    fs.writeFileSync(path.join(r.dir, 'b.txt'), 'b-committed\n')
    // Keep the stub and config out of the commits.
    fs.writeFileSync(path.join(r.dir, '.git', 'info', 'exclude'),
        'node_modules/\n.roast.jsonc\n')
    git(r.dir, r.env, 'add', '-A')
    git(r.dir, r.env, 'commit', '-qm', 'seed')
    fs.writeFileSync(path.join(r.dir, 'a.txt'), 'a-STAGED-NOT-COMMITTED\n')
    fs.writeFileSync(path.join(r.dir, 'b.txt'), 'b-IN-THIS-COMMIT\n')
    git(r.dir, r.env, 'add', 'a.txt')
    git(r.dir, r.env, 'config', '--local', 'roast.gate', 'enabled')
    return r
}

export async function enable (t:CleanupTest, r:GateRepo):Promise<void> {
    const res = await runRoast(['gate', 'enable'], { cwd:r.dir, env:r.env })
    t.equal(res.code, 0, `gate enable exits 0 ${res.stderr}`)
}

// A real path-scoped commit of b.txt.
export function commit (
    r:GateRepo,
    s:Stub,
    extra:Record<string, string> = {}
):{ ok:boolean, out:string } {
    const res = spawnSync('git',
        ['-C', r.dir, 'commit', 'b.txt', '-m', 'scoped'], {
            env:{ ...r.env, STUB_LOG:s.log, ...extra },
            encoding:'utf8',
        })
    return { ok:res.status === 0, out:res.stdout + res.stderr }
}

export function logLine (seen:string, prefix:string):string {
    const line = seen.split('\n').find(l => l.startsWith(prefix))
    return line === undefined ? '' : line.slice(prefix.length).trim()
}
import { test } from '@substrate-system/tapzero'
import fs from 'node:fs'
import os from 'node:os'
import path from 'node:path'
import { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process'
import { git, gitOk, gitRoot, GitError } from '../../src/git/git.js'

function tempDir (name:string):string {
    return fs.realpathSync(fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), name)))
}

function tempRepo ():string {
    const dir = tempDir('roast-git-')
    execFileSync('git', ['init', '-q', dir])
    return dir
}

// An env with only what git needs, so ambient config cannot leak in.
function baseEnv (extra:Record<string, string> = {}):NodeJS.ProcessEnv {
    return {
        PATH:process.env.PATH ?? '/usr/bin:/bin',
        HOME:process.env.HOME ?? '/',
        ...extra,
    }
}

function alive (pid:number):boolean {
    try {
        process.kill(pid, 0)
        return true
    } catch {
        return false
    }
}

test('gitRoot from a subdirectory returns the toplevel', async t => {
    const repo = tempRepo()
    const sub = path.join(repo, 'a', 'b')
    fs.mkdirSync(sub, { recursive:true })
    const root = await gitRoot(sub, { env:baseEnv() })
    t.equal(fs.realpathSync(root), fs.realpathSync(repo), 'toplevel')
    fs.rmSync(repo, { recursive:true, force:true })
})

test('gitRoot outside a repository fails', async t => {
    const dir = tempDir('roast-norepo-')
    const env = baseEnv({ GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES:path.dirname(dir) })
    let err:unknown
    try {
        await gitRoot(dir, { env })
    } catch (e) {
        err = e
    }
    t.ok(err instanceof Error, 'rejects')
    t.equal((err as Error)?.message, 'not inside a git repository',
        'not-in-a-repository error')
    fs.rmSync(dir, { recursive:true, force:true })
})

test('a failing git call rejects with a GitError', async t => {
    const repo = tempRepo()
    let err:unknown
    try {
        await git(repo, ['rev-parse', '--verify', 'no-such-ref'],
            { env:baseEnv() })
    } catch (e) {
        err = e
    }
    t.ok(err instanceof GitError, 'GitError')
    const gerr = err as GitError
    t.equal(gerr.exitCode, 128, 'exit code')
    t.ok(gerr.stderr.length > 0, 'stderr captured')
    t.equal(gerr.message, `exit status 128: ${gerr.stderr.trim()}`,
        'message is exit status and trimmed stderr')
    fs.rmSync(repo, { recursive:true, force:true })
})

test('git returns stdout as a Buffer and passes input to stdin', async t => {
    const repo = tempRepo()
    const out = await git(repo, ['hash-object', '--stdin'],
        { env:baseEnv(), input:'hello\n' })
    t.ok(Buffer.isBuffer(out), 'Buffer output')
    t.equal(out.toString().trim(),
        'ce013625030ba8dba906f756967f9e9ca394464a', 'blob hash of input')
    fs.rmSync(repo, { recursive:true, force:true })
})

test('gitOk resolves false on failure and true on success', async t => {
    const repo = tempRepo()
    const env = baseEnv()
    t.equal(await gitOk(repo, ['rev-parse', '--verify', '--quiet',
        'no-such-ref'], { env }), false, 'failing rev-parse --verify')
    t.equal(await gitOk(repo, ['rev-parse', '--git-dir'], { env }), true,
        'succeeding call')
    fs.rmSync(repo, { recursive:true, force:true })
})

test('the runner uses the given env and merges extra variables', async t => {
    const repo = tempRepo()
    const env = baseEnv({
        GIT_CONFIG_COUNT:'1',
        GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0:'roast.injected',
        GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0:'yes',
    })
    const out = await git(repo, ['config', '--get', 'roast.injected'],
        { env })
    t.equal(out.toString().trim(), 'yes', 'injected env reaches git')

    const extra = await git(repo, ['config', '--get', 'roast.extra'], {
        env:baseEnv(),
        extraEnv:{
            GIT_CONFIG_COUNT:'1',
            GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0:'roast.extra',
            GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0:'merged',
        },
    })
    t.equal(extra.toString().trim(), 'merged', 'extra env merged over env')

    // A variable only in process.env must not reach git when env is given.
    const saved = { ...process.env }
    process.env.GIT_CONFIG_COUNT = '1'
    process.env.GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0 = 'roast.leak'
    process.env.GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0 = 'leaked'
    try {
        t.equal(await gitOk(repo, ['config', '--get', 'roast.leak'],
            { env:baseEnv() }), false, 'process.env is not read')
    } finally {
        for (const k of ['GIT_CONFIG_COUNT', 'GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0',
            'GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0']) {
            if (saved[k] === undefined) delete process.env[k]
            else process.env[k] = saved[k]
        }
    }
    fs.rmSync(repo, { recursive:true, force:true })
})

// A fake `git` first on the injected PATH records its pid and then blocks.
// With `ignoreTerm`, it ignores SIGTERM so only SIGKILL stops it.
function fakeGit (ignoreTerm:boolean):{ bin:string, pidFile:string } {
    const bin = tempDir('roast-fakegit-')
    const pidFile = path.join(bin, 'pid')
    const trap = ignoreTerm ? "trap '' TERM\n" : ''
    fs.writeFileSync(path.join(bin, 'git'),
        `#!/bin/sh\n${trap}echo $$ > "${pidFile}"\nexec sleep 30\n`,
        { mode:0o755 })
    return { bin, pidFile }
}

async function waitFor (file:string):Promise<string> {
    for (let i = 0; i < 200; i++) {
        if (fs.existsSync(file)) {
            const text = fs.readFileSync(file, 'utf8').trim()
            if (text) return text
        }
        await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 10))
    }
    throw new Error(`timed out waiting for ${file}`)
}

for (const ignoreTerm of [false, true]) {
    const what = ignoreTerm ? ' that ignores SIGTERM' : ''
    test(`aborting a git call${what} rejects and kills the child`, async t => {
        const { bin, pidFile } = fakeGit(ignoreTerm)
        const ac = new AbortController()
        const reason = new Error('cancelled by test')
        const env = baseEnv({ PATH:`${bin}:${process.env.PATH}` })
        const call = git(bin, ['status'], { env, signal:ac.signal })
        const pid = Number(await waitFor(pidFile))
        t.ok(alive(pid), 'child is running')
        ac.abort(reason)
        let err:unknown
        try {
            await call
        } catch (e) {
            err = e
        }
        t.equal(err, reason, 'rejects with the abort reason')
        t.ok(!alive(pid), 'no child process left')
        fs.rmSync(bin, { recursive:true, force:true })
    })
}

test('an already aborted signal rejects without running git', async t => {
    const ac = new AbortController()
    const reason = new Error('early')
    ac.abort(reason)
    let err:unknown
    try {
        await gitOk(os.tmpdir(), ['status'],
            { env:baseEnv(), signal:ac.signal })
    } catch (e) {
        err = e
    }
    t.equal(err, reason, 'rejects with the reason')
})
// Temporary directories and Git repositories for CLI tests, with an
// environment that isolates the user's home and config directories and
// gives Git an identity (CI runners have none).
import { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process'
import fs from 'node:fs'
import os from 'node:os'
import path from 'node:path'
import { cleanEnv } from './env.js'

export interface TempDir {
    dir:string;
    env:Record<string, string>;
    remove ():void;
}

function makeTemp (prefix:string):string {
    return fs.realpathSync(fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), prefix)))
}

// The base test environment: the process env without provider or roast
// variables, with HOME, XDG_CONFIG_HOME, and CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR inside
// `root`, a Git identity, and no Git discovery above `root`.
export function isolatedEnv (root:string):Record<string, string> {
    const home = path.join(root, '.home')
    fs.mkdirSync(home, { recursive:true })
    return {
        ...cleanEnv(process.env),
        HOME:home,
        XDG_CONFIG_HOME:path.join(home, '.config'),
        CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:path.join(home, '.claude'),
        GIT_AUTHOR_NAME:'Roast Test',
        GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL:'roast-test@example.com',
        GIT_COMMITTER_NAME:'Roast Test',
        GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL:'roast-test@example.com',
        GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES:path.dirname(root),
        GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM:'1',
    }
}

// A temporary directory that is not inside any Git repository. The test
// working directory is `dir/work`.
export function tempDir (prefix = 'roast-dir-'):TempDir {
    const root = makeTemp(prefix)
    const dir = path.join(root, 'work')
    fs.mkdirSync(dir)
    return {
        dir,
        env:isolatedEnv(root),
        remove:() => fs.rmSync(root, { recursive:true, force:true }),
    }
}

// A temporary Git repository at `dir` (realpath, so it compares equal to
// what Git prints).
export function tempRepo (prefix = 'roast-repo-'):TempDir {
    const t = tempDir(prefix)
    execFileSync('git', ['init', '-q', t.dir], { env:t.env })
    return t
}
